WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:06.880
This is an incredible AI technique where in&nbsp;
goes a photo, and then, out comes a video of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:15.080
it. But wait, not any kind of video, even&nbsp;
better, a seamlessly looping video. I love&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.080 --> 00:00:21.000
it. What I found really surprising is that&nbsp;
it works quite well on a variety of cases,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.640 --> 00:00:30.400
not only on simple periodic movements for flowers,&nbsp;
but for even a sleeping cat, and more. Wow.

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:34.480
But, hold on to your flowers,&nbsp;
I mean papers Fellow Scholars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:44.400
because look. Boing. And a bigger one. And when&nbsp;
I tug here, a smaller one. These boings work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:53.680
on other scenes too. And all this little world&nbsp;
model from one simple image AI. Incredible. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.680 --> 00:01:00.840
not just animating the image, but we can&nbsp;
even play with it! How is this even possible?

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:07.720
This is an AI model that has looked at videos and&nbsp;
motion trajectories that happen in these videos,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:11.640
and based on that, it tries&nbsp;
to create something that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:17.760
call a neural stochastic motion texture.&nbsp;
These motion textures describe how pixels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:22.360
should move when they are tugged&nbsp;
at. How the boing should happen.

00:01:22.360 --> 00:01:29.400
But wait a second, if it has this kind of&nbsp;
representation of the motion, maybe it can also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:37.280
manipulate already existing videos too, right?&nbsp;
Oh yes, look! We can take an existing video,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:43.360
and even magnify the amount of motion that&nbsp;
is happening there. And I am very impressed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:48.240
I just kept looking and looking, and&nbsp;
couldn’t find a single issue with these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:53.760
videos. It would be very difficult to tell&nbsp;
that they aren’t real. And it gets better,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:59.840
it can even do a slow motion version of&nbsp;
already existing videos too! So good!

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:06.800
Previous techniques were not nearly as good as&nbsp;
this one in creating these animations. Look. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:15.320
image is trying to perform the impossible, which&nbsp;
is, showcase motion in one still image. So what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.320 --> 00:02:22.840
is happening here? Well, we take a small slice of&nbsp;
this photo and try to visualize how this slice is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.840 --> 00:02:30.280
moving over time. A previous technique did this,&nbsp;
stochastic I2V is from just 2 years ago, and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.280 --> 00:02:37.120
new technique. Okay, this looks better, but we&nbsp;
are experienced Fellow Scholars here. We wish&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:46.000
to see how it relates to the real motion. So…oh&nbsp;
my goodness, look at that. No wonder these videos&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:53.640
are so realistic, the synthesized motions are in&nbsp;
some cases, nearly indistinguishable from reality!

00:02:53.640 --> 00:03:00.200
Now, not even this technique is perfect. Look.&nbsp;
If I pull this away, I now have distorted the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.200 --> 00:03:10.560
original image a bit too much, and what is behind&nbsp;
here? We do not know. Information is missing. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:16.960
image inpainting techniques already exist,&nbsp;
so that can be remedied very easily. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.960 --> 00:03:25.080
the next one cannot, or at least, not easily.&nbsp;
Look. And the background also does not work here.

00:03:25.080 --> 00:03:29.440
Now, I think this will surprise you.&nbsp;
Being a computer graphics researcher,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:35.160
I have to note that there was an excellent&nbsp;
graphics paper, called Video Textures,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:41.960
which was a handcrafted technique that could do&nbsp;
something like this…yes you are seeing correctly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.960 --> 00:03:50.720
more than 20 years ago. Goodness! Then, the input&nbsp;
was a video and it tried to find connection points&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:57.000
where it could be connected back to the start of&nbsp;
the video and playing it again. Thus creating a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.000 --> 00:04:03.640
looping video from a non looping one. It was a&nbsp;
little more elaborate than that, but this was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.640 --> 00:04:11.840
the key idea. And it is a powerful idea. I wrote&nbsp;
my Bachelor thesis and a paper 15 years ago based&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.840 --> 00:04:17.440
on this technique, which was a procedural brush&nbsp;
stroke generation technique that could learn your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.440 --> 00:04:24.800
brushstrokes and apply it to already existing&nbsp;
works. It was a ton of fun and was made for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:31.840
feature-length movie. The paper and video of it&nbsp;
is available in the video description. And now, an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:39.920
animation from a single photo. Creamy, slow-motion&nbsp;
movement even! Love it. And just imagine what we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.920 --> 00:04:46.080
will be capable of just one more paper down&nbsp;
the line. My goodness. What a time to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.080 --> 00:05:51.480
alive! This was Two Minute Papers with Dr. Károly&nbsp;
Zsolnai-Fehér. Subscribe if you wish to see more.

